{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #13 — epic e369 (Jira DC) + parity epic 3e73. READ THIS, THEN #12, THEN #11. ===\n\nWORKTREE /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family. ALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\"\nDO NOT trust the board below — read the tickets. A spare worktree /Users/joeoakhart/agent-5200-diag\nis parked on current origin/main and is useful for closes (see the close trap below).\n\n--- WHAT LANDED THIS STRETCH ---\nMERGED: 1209 (727f+d103), 1214/1215/1216 (5200 tests), 1220/1221 (d067), 1222/1223/1224/1225 (cc77).\nCLOSED: 727f, d103. d067's close was IN FLIGHT at writing (see trap 1 — the first attempt was\nrejected and the retry is running from the spare worktree).\nFILED: 049e-9fac-a821-4ea2 (DC comment ceiling is admin-configurable; rebar hardcodes the default)\nand a3fa-e8d4-f4aa-4b51 (capability call sites are cast but not runtime-guarded). BOTH are\ndeliberately NOT e369 children — reasoning recorded on each; one command to overrule.\n\n*** d067 IS LIVE-PROVEN. THE DC OUTBOUND STATUS DEFECT IS FIXED AND CONFIRMED ON A REAL INSTANCE. ***\nHarness run 30765232591, job id 91542617555, main @ 2ea5c3391d: **1 failed, 48 passed**, with\n    [jira-live-canary] collected=49 executed=49 skipped=0\nand BOTH test_outbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status] and its inbound twin PASSED.\nThe immediately preceding run (30763838558, main @ 144bb02251, pre-fix) failed that same outbound\ncell with 'outbound status did not reach DC: fields.status.name is To Do'. Same harness, same\ninstance shape — a controlled before/after, not a bare green.\nThe ONLY remaining harness failure is 5200's assignee oracle failing its own SETUP precondition,\nwhich is that oracle correctly REFUSING to pass vacuously. An agent is fixing it.\n\n--- FOUR TRAPS THAT COST TIME. CARRY THEM. ---\n1. **A TICKET WITH DECLARED file_impact CANNOT BE CLOSED WITH `--ref`.** d067's close was rejected:\n   'it records file_impact (a code change) but no commit references it'. The trailers WERE correct\n   on both commits — the precheck searches for a referencing commit reachable from the WORKTREE's\n   HEAD, and `--ref <sha>` does NOT satisfy that precheck (it governs the completion verifier's\n   tree, not this check). 727f/d103 closed fine with `--ref` only because they had NO file_impact\n   declared. REMEDY: close from a worktree whose HEAD actually contains the commits — keep a spare\n   worktree fast-forwarded to origin/main for exactly this, rather than moving the HEAD of a\n   worktree that has an LLM gate running in it.\n2. **INSTALLING THE GERRIT commit-msg HOOK IN A WORKTREE CLOBBERS THE SHARED ONE — TWICE NOW.**\n   `git rev-parse --git-path hooks/commit-msg` resolves to the MAIN checkout's `.git/hooks`, so\n   `curl -sLo \"$(...)\"` overwrites pre-commit's CHAINED hook and silently kills the 50/72 message\n   gate for every worktree. That is why a 60-char subject passed local hooks earlier and only drew\n   a remote warning. DO NOT curl it. `make install` already wires it correctly: pre-commit's\n   commit-msg runs the message gate and chains Gerrit's as `commit-msg.legacy` for Change-Id\n   stamping. If it is already broken, `make hooks` repairs and VERIFIES all three gates.\n   NOTE: a fresh worktree genuinely may lack Change-Id stamping — verify with\n   `git log -1 --format=%b` after your first commit rather than pre-emptively curling.\n3. **A RUNTIME RELATIVE IMPORT OF THE PORT BREAKS 10 TESTS** ('attempted relative import with no\n   known parent package') because several test modules load reconciler files BY PATH. cc77's port\n   import must stay TYPE_CHECKING-guarded. This is the exact counterweight to cc77's own finding\n   that an ABSOLUTE `rebar_reconciler.` import is widened to `Any` by mypy: relative for typing,\n   guarded so it never executes. Also recorded on bd9e, whose dict import must stay ABSOLUTE for\n   the same spec_from_file_location reason.\n4. **`docs/env-vars.md` PINS LINE NUMBERS, so even a pure ANNOTATION change stales it** — that\n   produced a deterministic `Verified -1` on all four matrix jobs (no `recheck` would help). And\n   Gerrit verifies EACH change in a stack independently, so regenerating once at the tip is not\n   enough: regenerate inside the rebase (`git rebase --exec`).\n\n--- cc77 IS MERGED AND ITS RESULT IS WORTH KNOWING ---\n38 params annotated across 14 core modules. Exactly FOUR `[attr-defined]` findings, all opt-in\ncapability members, all resolved by narrowing (`cast(\"SupportsComments\"/\"SupportsLinks\", client)`)\n— none widened to `Any`, none reverted to the shim path, none added to TicketTransport's body. Cast\ntargets are STRINGS so the capability import stays under TYPE_CHECKING. One deliberate `Any`\nsurvives: `assert_transport_conforms(transport: Any)`, the runtime guard itself, pinned by an\nassertion so the exemption cannot grow into an allowlist. AC5's test was mutation-checked three\nways (bare / `Any` / shim-path import all go RED with the check's own message).\n`_TransportPortMeta`, `assert_transport_conforms` and the AST audit proven BYTE-IDENTICAL by\nAST-span comparison. dispatch_one was at 796/800 and did not fit; two PURE link-probe helpers moved\nto dispatch_apply_phases. A first attempt that also moved `_call_with_retry` was correctly\nABANDONED — 6 tests monkeypatch `dispatch_one.time`/`dispatch_one.RetryExhaustedError` as module\nglobals, so relocating it silently changes what those patches affect.\n\n--- THE 3e73 EPIC REVIEW TOOK FOUR ROUNDS AND EVERY BLOCK WAS REAL. DO NOT --force IT. ---\nThe operator explicitly ruled that --force is inappropriate while the gate is correcting genuine\ndeficiencies, and it was: (1) the epic had NO acceptance-criteria checklist at all; (2) the\nmutation-check AC was not `[operator-attested]` and named no evidence format — fixing it added a\nrequirement to record verdicts as a comment block IN THE TEST FILE, so the evidence is a durable\nrepo artifact instead of a claim that dies with the session; (3) an AC was a negative property with\nno deterministic check, now a positive one that ENUMERATES the two legitimate divergences; and (4)\ntwo findings against 79d5's plan, both verified in source and both serious:\n  * 79d5 said \"parametrized alongside Cloud's\". IMPOSSIBLE — Cloud's suites hardcode\n    `comment_limits.truncate_comment_body` (:154) and `ADF_PATH` / `_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`=32000\n    (:31-34), which have no DC equivalent. Following it literally means restructuring\n    incident-hardened Cloud tests, which this epic's own AC forbids. Now: SEPARATE DC files.\n  * **THE DC COMMENT TEST CANNOT REACH DC'S SANITIZER THROUGH `compute_outbound_mutations`.** That\n    function (outbound_differ.py:382-391) takes outbound_mapper/inbound_mapper/links but NO\n    sanitizer, and line 744 calls `_diff_comments(...)` WITHOUT forwarding one, so\n    `_resolve_sanitizer(None)` -> `select_backend(load_config()).sanitizer` = CLOUD. A DC comment\n    convergence test driven that way converges against CLOUD's ADF truncation while claiming to\n    prove DC. Remedy needs NO production change: `_diff_comments` already accepts `sanitizer=`\n    (outbound_comments.py:167, the ticket-21ca seam) — call it DIRECTLY with the DC sanitizer, and\n    inject `outbound_mapper=` for the description test. 79d5 now also requires a DISCRIMINATING\n    assertion (DC's plain-char fit + wiki suffix, which Cloud's ADF fit cannot produce) and a\n    mutation check that REMOVING the injection turns the cells red.\nLESSON: review CHILDREN BEFORE THE PARENT. An epic review pins its children's material, and a child\nbeing CERTIFIED mid-review invalidates it with a non-retryable\n`PlanReviewGenerationChanged` — `sign-review` is WRONG there; the tool tells you to re-review.\n\n--- STATE OF THE PARITY CHILDREN ---\ncedc, 2944, bd9e CERTIFIED. 79d5 in review round 3 after the fixes above. The 3e73 EPIC ITSELF is\nstill unsigned, and that is the ONLY thing blocking claims: the parent-first cascade needs the epic\nin_progress, which needs its attestation. Sequence: certify 79d5 -> review the epic LAST -> claim\nthe epic -> claim each child WITH HEAD PARKED AT THAT CHILD'S OWN attestation sha (they differ;\nthe claim gate compares HEAD to the attestation).\nFILE-CONFLICT MAP for parallel implementation: cedc / 2944 / 79d5 are mutually disjoint. bd9e\nCOLLIDES with 79d5 on `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` — serialize those two BY SCHEDULING,\nand do NOT add a depends_on edge (it would fast-fail the other's review and stale attestations)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785704966859689001,"uuid":"cd51cdb4-92c2-4f1c-955f-a41256c646be"}